00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.400 align:start position:0%
 
one other house of commons was  captivated last week when the Prime  Minister launched what's been seen as a  thinly veiled attack on the Mayor of  London Boris Johnson over his decision  to back the brexit camp and there's a  warning in the Sunday Times this morning  that David Cameron could face a  leadership challenge if the so-called  blue on blue attacks don't stop well  Chris Grayling the leader of the house  is one of the cabinet ministers company  to leave the EU and he joins me now from  sky center and a very good morning to  you Mr grayling morning noogie said in  any way ironic that the initial warning  when the campaign kind of began about  blue on blue attacks so-called came from  Downing Street they said they didn't  want to see any of that and yet the  Prime Minister seems to be the first to  carry one out well the important thing  is absolutely that point we're going to  go through four months of debate there  are different opinions in the party  everyone has got to go out of their way  to make sure that the debate within the  Conservative Party is a constructive one  an amicable one and one that doesn't  involve attacks on each other that's  really important so you do think the  Prime Minister made it personal then  well I mean I you can interpret the  words for Prime Minister use on Monday  at the number of different ways and I  don't want to come pick on that  particular example and say that's right  that's wrong I think going forward we've  got to make ish make sure that nobody  who is involved in the debate act in a  way that is hostile to any other member  of the party frankly that's true the  broader debate the country as a whole  wants to make its mind up on the basis  of hard facts on the basis of proper  information and evidence and none of us  go to win support by actually turning  this into a battle run the debate but  the Prime Minister I mean what was you  were sitting there what do you think  when you heard the Prime Minister he  seemed to be angered did he go over the  top well I'm he was setting out a point  of debates and you can interpret that  whichever way you want what I'm saying  is going forward we've got to make sure  that none of us do anything that is  easily interpreted as an attack on each  other we've got to be very careful to  make sure within the party and indeed  outside the party that this is a debate  that is measured constructive sensible  and of course from a conservative point  of view after the event we've really got  to make sure that when the result comes  in we come together we heal the party we  move on beyond the divisions and  discussions at the moment and we win in  2020  it isn't just that isn't it isn't just  what we all got to see in the House of  Commons then coming from deep we suppose  within the remain camp this suggestion  that the justice secretary michael gove  that he might face the the sack if your  side loses well look I have said I don't  believe at the moment the Prime Minister  is sitting in Downing Street plotting  sackings of ministers I mean it would  not be at all sensible when the  referendum is over for there to be a  purge of people on either side of the  argument if we are going to come  together if we're going to work  harmonious is a party and if above all  we're going to beat the Labour Party and  winning 2020 we've got to come together  well and there cannot be recriminations  at the end of this the Prime Minister's  done the right thing in giving us all  the freedom to debate on one side of the  argument or the other and I really  admire him for doing that all of us  involved in that debate must make sure  afterwards there are no recriminations  but don't you think they've been  surprised i mean there's the other  report they've been surprised with in  the conservative remain camp about that  the scale of the opposition to them from  within the party and from within the  cabinet well I'm not surprised there's a  lot of conservatives who want to leave  the European Union there's a lot of  people across the country you want to  leave the European Union I was out in  the Midlands yesterday in a number of  different places and getting a pretty  consistent message people coming up to  me saying we want to leave so I'm not  surprised there are strong feelings in  favor of leave in the Conservative Party  but what matters is for the next four  months we argue our case we set out our  views we try and win the arguments we  have the result and then we come  together because what we cannot do is  let Jeremy Corbyn anywhere near number  ten Downing Street but I mean just  nevertheless last thought on this kind  of issue of it that mean just how  important is the issue for you  personally if it turns out to be a  career-defining or indeed career  destroying moment you feel it's a price  worth paying well I'm not in this  because I'm trying to keep my place in  the cabinet or I'm trying to put in  danger my place in the cabinet I'm doing  this because I think it's the right  thing to do I genuinely believe Britain  would be better off outside the european  union that would be far more  opportunities for us in the world it  would open up new avenues for the United  Kingdom enable us to govern ourselves  again and that to me is more important  than my job it's more important than the  role I fell in cabinet and I think  that's true of all of us who are  campaigning to leave your opinion in  we're not thinking about our careers but  I  towards and I don't from moment think  that the Prime Minister is sitting in  Downing Street planning some kind of  Acts session it would be hardly the best  way of bringing the party together  afterwards and I don't from moment think  he's he's planning that at the moment  but passions are running high you've got  to admit that we hear that the Foreign  Secretary had some choice words let me  buddy that way to say about Savile cash  what passions often run high and debate  but they mustn't be lasting passions  we've got to make sure that we stay  friendly we stay constructive we come  together when the referendum is over and  what about this issue that I mean tell  me how it works with you this idea then  it's not an idea it's it's a diktat  coming from sir jeremy heywood head of  the civil service saying that ministers  like you shouldn't have access to the  civil servants if you're using the  information on the leave campaign well i  think the civil service machines and  everyone's could be very careful over  the next four months because government  has to continue we will have european  meetings we will have european documents  crossing our desks we will have to as  ministers take decisions about the right  way forward for the United Kingdom even  through the referendum period and so it  is really important that whatever  happens ministers can carry on running  their departments ministers can carry on  doing the right job for the United  Kingdom but of course nobody's actually  expecting when the Prime Minister is on  a different side of the argument for us  to be getting kind of detailed briefings  about what he's planning to do in the  days and weeks ahead yeah but if you  want some basic information about how  one of your proposals might be affected  whether we're in or out of the European  Union you want to use some of the  resources don't you of the of the civil  service the Prime Minister presumably  could tell Sir Jeremy to to change his  view on this couldn't Lee well I think  it's really actually important that on  both sides of the argument we are seen  not to use in appropriate amounts of  public money in appropriate amounts of  public time to put together information  that is going to be used as part of the  argument for the referendum now the  government has a formal position on this  and so of course the government and the  Prime Minister are going to argue that  formal position but the public will not  thank us if they see us wasting public  money as part of the debate process and  do you accept now that as the the Prime  Minister made very clear during the  course of law  last week that this referendum is the  final decision that there would be no  shilly-shallying afterwards and maybe  hoping that the EU makes up its mind if  I gone we're gone well I'm very clear if  we vote to leave we leave what then  follows is a discussion about our future  relationship with the European Union and  of course we will carry on trading with  them there a whole range of scare  stories around at the moment somehow we  know the world is going to change in a  way that's going to destroy businesses  destroy jobs the actual truth is that we  buy far more from the European Union  than they buy from us if anybody is  worried about the outcome at the moment  its continental businesses in France in  Germany and Spain and Italy who will not  want to see trading arrangements change  and that is why we'll carry on trading  we're carrying a habit of having the  opportunity to sell our products into  the European Union because they're not  going to put in danger the ability to  sell cars from Germany into Britain or  agricultural products cheese and wine  from france since britain that's not  going to happen but you won't accept  wouldn't you that the the leave side  have have a difficulty in painting for  the voting public what precisely the one  vision of britain post brexit would look  like when you've got everything from  george galloway socialist paradise to  nigel farage is red in tooth and claw  capitalist paradise what are people  going to make of it we're hearing that  the romaine campaign are going to be  asking those very questions about the 25  scenarios to consider from the leave  side well there's an awful lot of talk  from the romaine side about well you  want to be the Norwegian model you want  to be the Turkish model the Canadian  model I want us to be the United Kingdom  model we will negotiate our own  arrangements with the European Union we  will do so because we buy far more from  them than they buy from us and so  therefore they need us they need to  trade with us we will not accept  unlimited free movement in the way that  we have had up to now but nor are we  going to stop people coming in living  and working in other countries in the  right and proper way in a managed way  which we don't have at the moment but  these will be the subject of a detailed  negotiation when the time comes but when  it comes to the groups of people who are  campaigning yes of course there's a very  diverse group of people campaigning but  ultimately it will be a Conservative  government that leads us out the  European Union that leads us into a  relationship based on free trade or  brought a look around the world on new  relationships with emerging economies in  different parts of the world that can  create jobs and opportunities for people  and businesses here okay leader has  great talking to thank you very much  indeed Chris Grayling there

